<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!-- ===================================================================================
         V1 BASELINE (SCRUM-107, owner directive 2026-08-08). THIS IS THE FIRST MIGRATION
         man_isr_resp WILL EVER SEE.

         Every DCRE database is dropped and recreated for the direct cut-over, so there is
         no historic state, no MAR-era copy of this table and nothing to converge onto. The
         retrofit apparatus a migration needs is ABSENT BY DESIGN and must not be re-added:
         no <validCheckSum>ANY</validCheckSum> (no checksum was ever recorded anywhere), no
         defensive IF NOT EXISTS on the CREATE, no create-then-drop pair. The changeset-identity
         note this file used to carry, about the table changing owning service from mar to mix
         and therefore already standing on a live database, describes a database that no longer
         exists.

         THE TWO MARK_RAN GUARDS BELOW ARE CONVERGENCE GUARDS, NOT RETROFITS. man_isr_resp has
         TWO creators on a brand-new dcre_man: this file, and MRG's bootstrap pre-create in
         mrg 004-man-views.xml (changeset 004-bootstrap-man-isr-resp-mrg), which mints the table
         and the unique constraint together because MRG is clock-launched and may run before MIX
         has ever executed. All ten M-services migrate the one dcre_man with ten independent
         history + lock tables and NOTHING serializes them, so whichever of MIX and MRG runs
         first wins and the other side's guarded changesets MARK_RAN. That race, not any migrated
         database, is the entire reason these guards exist.

         MIX is the single RUNTIME writer of man_isr_resp, the pain.012 ISR acceptance-leg
         response table, and of NOTHING else. The sbsr and pbsr tables live with their own
         services (MSX, MPX): one reader per leg, the exact mirror of the collections cix/csx/cpx
         fleet. The merged three-table changelog was MAR's shape and is the deviation this
         refactor removes, so the reply.type launch arg is gone too: the leg is a compile-time
         property of the service.

         One row per reply file (a pain.012 message carries exactly ONE mandate,
         unlike the collections ISR fan-out). Replay-safe and resume-safe via
         UNIQUE (response_file, mndt_req_id): the FULL business identity of a
         landed reply row (the file it came in plus the mandate it answers), so
         the guarded INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (response_file, mndt_req_id) DO NOTHING
         is a zero-duplicate no-op on re-run (never UPSERT on the PK; CRDB resolves
         UPSERT on PK only, persistence.md).
         orgnl_msg_id = the outbound MsgId MIX correlates to man_outbound.out_msg_id
         (fail-closed: an unknown outbound identity is WARNed and excluded, never
         guessed, SCRUM-60 canon). mndt_id = the MndtId (mandate_ref), mndt_req_id =
         the MRR-minted MndtReqId. e2e is nullable-but-forward-compatible: the
         current synthetic pain.012 contract (A-60) carries no EndToEndId element,
         so it is parsed opportunistically and stays NULL until the reply shape
         carries it. status/reason are the literal MndtSts/Rsn code values (R-21:
         code values, never enum ordinals).
         =================================================================================== -->

    <changeSet id="mix-001-man-isr-resp" author="dcre">
        <!-- CONVERGENCE GUARD: MRG's 004-bootstrap-man-isr-resp-mrg pre-creates this table
             (with its unique constraint) when MRG's clock launch beats MIX's first run on a
             brand-new dcre_man. Nothing serializes the ten M-service migrations, so either
             order is legal and the loser MARK_RANs. See the file header. -->
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not><tableExists tableName="man_isr_resp"/></not>
        </preConditions>
        <createTable tableName="man_isr_resp">
            <column name="id" type="UUID" defaultValueComputed="gen_random_uuid()">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="response_file" type="VARCHAR(128)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="orgnl_msg_id" type="VARCHAR(35)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="mndt_id" type="VARCHAR(35)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="mndt_req_id" type="VARCHAR(35)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="e2e" type="VARCHAR(35)"/>
            <column name="status" type="VARCHAR(8)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="reason" type="VARCHAR(8)"/>
            <column name="version" type="BIGINT" defaultValueNumeric="0"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="created_at" type="TIMESTAMPTZ" defaultValueComputed="now()">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="updated_at" type="TIMESTAMPTZ" defaultValueComputed="now()">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </createTable>
    </changeSet>

    <!-- CONVERGENCE GUARD, second half. The unique constraint is its OWN changeset guarded on
         its OWN schema state (persistence.md: guard each step on the schema state it transforms,
         not on file order), so MIX converges on whatever part of the shape the other creator put
         in place. Today MRG mints table and constraint together, so both of these changesets
         MARK_RAN as a pair when MRG wins the race; the split exists so that a creator which ever
         lands the table alone still gets the constraint. Folded into the createTable changeset
         above, the single tableExists gate would MARK_RAN BOTH statements against a table
         standing without its constraint, and then every runtime
         ON CONFLICT (response_file, mndt_req_id) dies with "there is no unique or exclusion
         constraint matching the ON CONFLICT specification": the zero-duplicate idempotency
         guarantee silently gone. A UNIQUE constraint materialises as an index of the same name,
         so indexExists is the schema-state probe for it. Both orders and the table-only partial
         are proven in MixConvergenceIT (SCRUM-91 review R5, v1 recast SCRUM-107). -->
    <changeSet id="mix-001-man-isr-resp-uq" author="dcre">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not><indexExists tableName="man_isr_resp" indexName="uq_man_isr_resp_file_mndt_req"/></not>
        </preConditions>
        <addUniqueConstraint tableName="man_isr_resp" columnNames="response_file, mndt_req_id"
                             constraintName="uq_man_isr_resp_file_mndt_req"/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
